<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baalshamin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Baal Shamin, Baalshamem</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baalshamin, 'Lord of Heaven', was a Semitic sky and weather god worshipped across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> from the early 1st millennium BCE to late antiquity. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> stele of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakkur">Zakkur</a>, king of Hamath, from around 800 BCE thanks him for saving the king from a siege, and later he had temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>, Hatra and elsewhere. His temple at Palmyra was destroyed by the Islamic State group in 2015.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baalshamin.jpg" alt="Baalshamin" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PalmyreneDeities.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Baalshamin means Lord of Heaven or Heavens, written Baʿal Šāmīn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and Baʿl Šāmēm in Phoenician. It was a god and also a title applied to different gods at different places and times in ancient Middle Eastern inscriptions, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. It was most often applied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Hadad</a>, who is also often called simply <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>. The name was originally a title of Baal Hadad in the 2nd millennium BCE and came to designate a distinct god around 1000 BCE. The earliest known Phoenician reference is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yehimilk">Yehimilk</a> inscription of the 10th century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A treaty of the 14th century BCE between the Hittite king Suppiluliumas I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niqmaddu-ii">Niqmaddu II</a> of Ugarit may mention the god, though this could refer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal Hadad</a>. In the treaty of 677 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and Baal I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, a curse calls on Baal-sameme, Baal-malage and Baal-saphon to raise an evil wind against the ships of Tyre. Interpreters disagree whether these are three gods or three aspects of one god who causes stormy weather at sea. Inscriptions show that the cult continued at Tyre from Esarhaddon's day until towards the end of the 1st millennium BCE.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> in Syria Baalshamin was one of the two supreme gods, with Bel. His attributes there were the eagle and the lightning bolt, and he perhaps formed a triad with the moon god Aglibol and the sun god Malakbel. Nabataean texts in Greek equate him with Zeus Helios, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> writers call him Zeus Olympios. The Syriac writer Jacob of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saruj">Serugh</a> lists him among the idols of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Among the nomads of pre-Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, Baʿal-Samīn was the central figure of Safaitic prayers for rain. The name is not of Arabic origin: the element smn adapts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> word for the heavens. He is often called god of Seia, a major sanctuary in the Ḥawrān region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. He holds an almost exclusive control of precipitation in these inscriptions, was credited with giving healing properties to pools of rainwater, and was offered blood sacrifice to induce him to release rain.</p><p>The sources do not make clear whether he was regarded as bringer of rain or as cause of drought. In one inscription a worshipper denounces him as unjust before an assembly of seven other deities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baalshamin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Astarte, goddess of love, fertility and war, was widely worshipped, and Baal, “lord”, was a title borne by several gods, among them <b>Baalshamin</b>, “lord of heaven”.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 800 BCE the usurper Zakkur ruled Hamath and Lu'ash, and his Aramaic stele tells how the god <b>Baalshamin</b> saved him from a siege by a coalition led by Aram-Damascus.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamath">Hamath</a></li><li><blockquote>At its centre stood a vast temple precinct to the sun god Shamash, called Maran ('our lord'), with shrines to Allat, <b>Baalshamin</b> and other deities.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hatra">Kingdom of Hatra</a></li><li><blockquote>His Aramaic stele, found at Tell Afis, thanks the god <b>Baalshamin</b> for deliverance.</blockquote><a href="/state/luash">Lu'ash</a></li></ul></section>
